WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:11.374
DG32-LITE is Deepgrid Semi's motor-control chip, and it makes one argument: hardware lockstep safety no longer needs an automotive-class microcontroller.

2
00:00:11.924 --> 00:00:18.214
Two RISC-V cores check each other on every write, inside a nine-by-nine millimetre package.

3
00:00:18.764 --> 00:00:30.501
First silicon rides the September 2026 multi-project shuttle, so every figure you will see is a design value, verified in simulation and timing analysis.

4
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.342
Here is the whole story on one slide.

5
00:00:34.892 --> 00:00:42.515
One 130 nanometre chip carries the microcontroller, the motor-control peripherals and a hardware safety monitor.

6
00:00:43.065 --> 00:00:45.339
The two cores run in lockstep.

7
00:00:45.889 --> 00:00:58.015
The current loop reaches about one hundred kilohertz in simulation, and a CPU disagreement latches within thirty-nine cycles and drives a fault pin that can switch the power bridge off.

8
00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:00.693
Why build it this way?

9
00:01:01.243 --> 00:01:05.825
A motor drive switches power transistors thousands of times a second.

10
00:01:06.375 --> 00:01:14.706
If the CPU silently computes a wrong value, it writes a wrong PWM edge, and a wrong edge can short a bridge leg.

11
00:01:15.256 --> 00:01:21.501
Entry-level motor MCUs catch faults with watchdogs and periodic software self-test.

12
00:01:22.051 --> 00:01:28.534
Hardware lockstep has lived in automotive parts such as AURIX, S32K and Hercules.

13
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.425
DG32-LITE is six block groups on one bus.

14
00:01:33.975 --> 00:01:41.319
The safety core, memory and supervision sit above it; motor drive, sensing, connectivity and test sit below.

15
00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:47.577
The bus has two masters, the CPU and a DMA, with deterministic latency.

16
00:01:48.127 --> 00:01:56.821
Any address that is unmapped or disabled answers with a bus error instead of hanging, which matters on a chip with no debugger halt.

17
00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.861
Four hard constraints shaped every block.

18
00:02:01.411 --> 00:02:04.561
There is no debugger halt, so nothing may hang.

19
00:02:05.111 --> 00:02:11.622
The lockstep core tops out around fifty-five to sixty-two megahertz, so the die runs at fifty.

20
00:02:12.172 --> 00:02:17.892
The core costs about eight clock cycles per instruction, so the loop moves into hardware.

21
00:02:18.442 --> 00:02:24.607
And the memory macros have no spare bits, so the chip ships without ECC, and says so.

22
00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.485
This is the safety core.

23
00:02:28.035 --> 00:02:30.379
MAIN runs the application.

24
00:02:30.929 --> 00:02:38.518
CHECKER runs the same instructions two cycles later, on the same inputs, and the comparator checks every committed store.

25
00:02:39.068 --> 00:02:43.139
On a mismatch, the first cause is latched and FAULT_N goes low.

26
00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:50.010
Route that pin to the gate-driver enable, and the bridge turns off in hardware, without waiting for firmware.

27
00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.775
The chip boots itself.

28
00:02:53.325 --> 00:03:04.879
At power-on the mask ROM prints a banner, validates the flash image header, copies the application from external QSPI flash into SRAM, and jumps to it.

29
00:03:05.429 --> 00:03:12.312
If the flash is blank, the ROM starts a UART monitor instead, so a bare board can still be inspected.

30
00:03:12.862 --> 00:03:16.938
The trade-off is loading at boot instead of executing in place.

31
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:23.401
Every expensive step of field-oriented control runs in a dedicated block.

32
00:03:23.951 --> 00:03:27.975
Encoder and Hall inputs give position, with edge timestamps.

33
00:03:28.525 --> 00:03:34.224
The PWM fires the ADC at the centre of each period, where ripple is lowest.

34
00:03:34.774 --> 00:03:46.671
The CORDIC computes the Park transforms, the CPU runs only the two PI regulators, and the PWM block applies the new duty cycles, with a hardware brake behind it.

35
00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.760
Here is what one loop costs.

36
00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.977
The current sample takes one hundred seventy-seven cycles.

37
00:03:54.527 --> 00:03:58.010
Each CORDIC operation takes fifty-three to fifty-eight.

38
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.964
The regulators cost about eight cycles per instruction.

39
00:04:02.514 --> 00:04:08.385
Together the hardware part is about three hundred cycles, and it is the same at every loop rate.

40
00:04:08.935 --> 00:04:12.296
At twenty kilohertz, that is twelve percent of the period.

41
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.467
So the question becomes how much firmware fits.

42
00:04:17.017 --> 00:04:24.770
At ten kilohertz there are about forty-seven hundred cycles left: enough for a full current and speed loop with an observer.

43
00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.355
At twenty kilohertz, twenty-two hundred, enough for field-weakening.

44
00:04:29.905 --> 00:04:33.891
At fifty kilohertz, seven hundred, an inner current loop only.

45
00:04:34.441 --> 00:04:37.808
The simulated ceiling is about one hundred kilohertz.

46
00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.947
Timing tells the same story.

47
00:04:41.497 --> 00:04:53.352
After place-and-route, the GPIO, DShot, QSPI and PWM blocks all run above one hundred sixty-five megahertz, and CORDIC and the supervisor above ninety.

48
00:04:53.902 --> 00:04:58.756
Only the lockstep core, at fifty-five to sixty-two megahertz, sets the clock.

49
00:04:59.306 --> 00:05:01.909
No peripheral is ever the timing wall.

50
00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:12.334
All of this fits in a nine-by-nine millimetre QFN with sixty-four pins: forty-four signals, and the rest supplies and ground.

51
00:05:12.884 --> 00:05:21.754
Seven pins drive the bridge, six read position, two carry the analog current input, and one, FAULT_N, is the hardware trip.

52
00:05:22.304 --> 00:05:27.825
The compute variant, DG32-2DOM, uses exactly the same pinout.

53
00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:39.309
DG32-2DOM keeps everything in DG32-LITE and adds an INT8 attention engine on its own one hundred fourteen megahertz clock.

54
00:05:39.859 --> 00:05:46.621
It reaches memory through clock-domain-crossing bridges, so it never stalls the fifty megahertz control core.

55
00:05:47.171 --> 00:05:52.389
Its output is bit-exact to the software model, and timing closes at both clocks.

56
00:05:52.939 --> 00:05:57.624
It is designed for bearing-fault and anomaly detection inside the drive.

57
00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:05.191
Against the STM32G0, the entry-level incumbent, the comparison is honest.

58
00:06:05.741 --> 00:06:14.873
DG32 leads on hardware lockstep, native DShot, hardware CORDIC and the on-chip AI variant, with an open RISC-V core.

59
00:06:15.423 --> 00:06:24.697
The G0 leads clearly on its twelve-bit multi-channel ADC, embedded flash, USB and CAN-FD, and on production maturity.

60
00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.632
The roadmap closes those gaps in order.

61
00:06:29.182 --> 00:06:35.442
First silicon on the September 2026 shuttle measures what simulation predicted.

62
00:06:35.992 --> 00:06:41.043
The second spin adds a twelve-bit multi-channel ADC and embedded flash.

63
00:06:41.593 --> 00:06:45.292
Then come CAN-FD and interactive CPU debug.

64
00:06:45.842 --> 00:06:51.971
DG32-2DOM runs in parallel, with its design complete and in physical trials.

65
00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:55.022
Three things to take away.

66
00:06:55.572 --> 00:06:59.663
Safety is in the core: two CPUs must agree, in hardware.

67
00:07:00.213 --> 00:07:04.156
Control is in silicon: the loop cost is fixed and known.

68
00:07:04.706 --> 00:07:10.976
And the next milestone is measurement: first silicon turns these design values into measured ones.

69
00:07:11.526 --> 00:07:17.009
This deck claims no measured silicon results and no functional-safety certification yet.

